{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"temporal-persistence-v2","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Temporal persistence v2"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The implemented design preserves native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Temporal.Instant"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Temporal.PlainDate"},{"type":"text","value":" values through the existing SQLite query pipeline. It keeps\nSQL filtering and index use, reconstructs native values, then uses the existing\nresidual filtering, ordering and pagination stages."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The grammar is:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"supported value × carrier × operation × legal history × execution boundary","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Its selected rules are concrete:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use registered global Temporal constructors for brand validation and native\nreconstruction. Preserve both kind and value; reject missing constructors and\nother Temporal kinds. Strings remain strings."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Encode native values consistently across rows, nested data, metadata and replay.\nEscape an ordinary record's marker field without moving its other JSON fields."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate lossless canonical text from sortable keys. Instant keys retain\nnanoseconds; PlainDate keys preserve ISO chronological order across calendars\nand signed years. Equality additionally retains kind and calendar text."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Share reference/literal representations between runtime SQL and persisted\nindexes. Exact equality and correlated IN membership remain safe under NOT.\nCoalesce retains both representations; existing SQL fallbacks and final cleanup\nremain. Ordered comparisons require compatible same-kind operands."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve action validation, durable rollback, wrapper receipts/FIFO and the\npublication-before-durability boundary. Reuse existing oracle owners."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retain the narrower remote-subset wire domain and reject permanent input\nerrors before retry admission. Multiprocess native-value support is not added."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The wrapper oracle exposed an additional receiving boundary: Collection index\nmetadata previously JSON-cloned native literals into strings and gave distinct\nTemporal literals the same signature. The implementation now preserves native\nliterals while copying the expression structure and includes kind/text in the\nsignature. Its test observes metadata, emitted SQL and actual index use."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The original implementation failed 33 targeted expanded witnesses before the final routing and scalar-index additions. A deliberately\nwrong lexical-order implementation fails eight cases while still reconstructing\nnative values correctly. The routing witnesses also catch a request that becomes remote during hydration; valid requests now dispatch exactly once. Small and large membership indexes retain SQLite compatibility. The repaired affected suite reports "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"471 passed,\n1 existing TODO, 0 failed"}]},{"type":"text","value":". TypeScript and lint pass; lint retains existing\nrequire-await warnings. Full results, source hashes and the oracle audit are in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"v2-evidence.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"evidence"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Old data may be replaced, as the user specified. This additive implementation\ndoes not automatically erase it: existing schema-version/reset options can\nreplace already-damaged caches. Empty objects cannot reveal their former type,\nand older readers cannot interpret the new native encoding."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The claim is bounded to the reached paths and histories. Engine-native\nconstructors, native mobile/browser runs, multiprocess row transport,\ntruncate/delete histories with native values and arbitrary Temporal operations\nremain unproved. Independently held-out range evidence and formal model checking are absent; host behavior and untested schedules remain a decomposition limit. Index-plan checks establish index use, not a latency guarantee."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The detailed "},{"type":"link","href":"v2-model.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"model"}]},{"type":"text","value":" preserves the initial frozen rules and explicitly\ndated implementation discoveries. "},{"type":"link","href":"v2-process.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Process"}]},{"type":"text","value":" records reconstruction,\nablation, controls and the projection map. No new lifecycle model or generic\ncodec framework was introduced."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"temporal-persistence-v2","text":"Temporal persistence v2","level":1}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Temporal persistence v2\n\nThe implemented design preserves native `Temporal.Instant` and\n`Temporal.PlainDate` values through the existing SQLite query pipeline. It keeps\nSQL filtering and index use, reconstructs native values, then uses the existing\nresidual filtering, ordering and pagination stages.\n\nThe grammar is:\n\n```text\nsupported value × carrier × operation × legal history × execution boundary\n```\n\nIts selected rules are concrete:\n\n- Use registered global Temporal constructors for brand validation and native\n  reconstruction. Preserve both kind and value; reject missing constructors and\n  other Temporal kinds. Strings remain strings.\n- Encode native values consistently across rows, nested data, metadata and replay.\n  Escape an ordinary record's marker field without moving its other JSON fields.\n- Separate lossless canonical text from sortable keys. Instant keys retain\n  nanoseconds; PlainDate keys preserve ISO chronological order across calendars\n  and signed years. Equality additionally retains kind and calendar text.\n- Share reference/literal representations between runtime SQL and persisted\n  indexes. Exact equality and correlated IN membership remain safe under NOT.\n  Coalesce retains both representations; existing SQL fallbacks and final cleanup\n  remain. Ordered comparisons require compatible same-kind operands.\n- Preserve action validation, durable rollback, wrapper receipts/FIFO and the\n  publication-before-durability boundary. Reuse existing oracle owners.\n- Retain the narrower remote-subset wire domain and reject permanent input\n  errors before retry admission. Multiprocess native-value support is not added.\n\nThe wrapper oracle exposed an additional receiving boundary: Collection index\nmetadata previously JSON-cloned native literals into strings and gave distinct\nTemporal literals the same signature. The implementation now preserves native\nliterals while copying the expression structure and includes kind/text in the\nsignature. Its test observes metadata, emitted SQL and actual index use.\n\nThe original implementation failed 33 targeted expanded witnesses before the final routing and scalar-index additions. A deliberately\nwrong lexical-order implementation fails eight cases while still reconstructing\nnative values correctly. The routing witnesses also catch a request that becomes remote during hydration; valid requests now dispatch exactly once. Small and large membership indexes retain SQLite compatibility. The repaired affected suite reports **471 passed,\n1 existing TODO, 0 failed**. TypeScript and lint pass; lint retains existing\nrequire-await warnings. Full results, source hashes and the oracle audit are in\n[evidence](v2-evidence.md).\n\nOld data may be replaced, as the user specified. This additive implementation\ndoes not automatically erase it: existing schema-version/reset options can\nreplace already-damaged caches. Empty objects cannot reveal their former type,\nand older readers cannot interpret the new native encoding.\n\nThe claim is bounded to the reached paths and histories. Engine-native\nconstructors, native mobile/browser runs, multiprocess row transport,\ntruncate/delete histories with native values and arbitrary Temporal operations\nremain unproved. Independently held-out range evidence and formal model checking are absent; host behavior and untested schedules remain a decomposition limit. Index-plan checks establish index use, not a latency guarantee.\n\nThe detailed [model](v2-model.md) preserves the initial frozen rules and explicitly\ndated implementation discoveries. [Process](v2-process.md) records reconstruction,\nablation, controls and the projection map. No new lifecycle model or generic\ncodec framework was introduced.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Temporal persistence v2\n\nThe implemented design preserves native `Temporal.Instant` and\n`Temporal.PlainDate` values through the existing SQLite query pipeline. It keeps\nSQL filtering and index use, reconstructs native values, then uses the existing\nresidual filtering, ordering and pagination stages.\n\nThe grammar is:\n\n```text\nsupported value × carrier × operation × legal history × execution boundary\n```\n\nIts selected rules are concrete:\n\n- Use registered global Temporal constructors for brand validation and native\n  reconstruction. Preserve both kind and value; reject missing constructors and\n  other Temporal kinds. Strings remain strings.\n- Encode native values consistently across rows, nested data, metadata and replay.\n  Escape an ordinary record's marker field without moving its other JSON fields.\n- Separate lossless canonical text from sortable keys. Instant keys retain\n  nanoseconds; PlainDate keys preserve ISO chronological order across calendars\n  and signed years. Equality additionally retains kind and calendar text.\n- Share reference/literal representations between runtime SQL and persisted\n  indexes. Exact equality and correlated IN membership remain safe under NOT.\n  Coalesce retains both representations; existing SQL fallbacks and final cleanup\n  remain. Ordered comparisons require compatible same-kind operands.\n- Preserve action validation, durable rollback, wrapper receipts/FIFO and the\n  publication-before-durability boundary. Reuse existing oracle owners.\n- Retain the narrower remote-subset wire domain and reject permanent input\n  errors before retry admission. Multiprocess native-value support is not added.\n\nThe wrapper oracle exposed an additional receiving boundary: Collection index\nmetadata previously JSON-cloned native literals into strings and gave distinct\nTemporal literals the same signature. The implementation now preserves native\nliterals while copying the expression structure and includes kind/text in the\nsignature. Its test observes metadata, emitted SQL and actual index use.\n\nThe original implementation failed 33 targeted expanded witnesses before the final routing and scalar-index additions. A deliberately\nwrong lexical-order implementation fails eight cases while still reconstructing\nnative values correctly. The routing witnesses also catch a request that becomes remote during hydration; valid requests now dispatch exactly once. Small and large membership indexes retain SQLite compatibility. The repaired affected suite reports **471 passed,\n1 existing TODO, 0 failed**. TypeScript and lint pass; lint retains existing\nrequire-await warnings. Full results, source hashes and the oracle audit are in\n[evidence](v2-evidence.md).\n\nOld data may be replaced, as the user specified. This additive implementation\ndoes not automatically erase it: existing schema-version/reset options can\nreplace already-damaged caches. Empty objects cannot reveal their former type,\nand older readers cannot interpret the new native encoding.\n\nThe claim is bounded to the reached paths and histories. Engine-native\nconstructors, native mobile/browser runs, multiprocess row transport,\ntruncate/delete histories with native values and arbitrary Temporal operations\nremain unproved. Independently held-out range evidence and formal model checking are absent; host behavior and untested schedules remain a decomposition limit. Index-plan checks establish index use, not a latency guarantee.\n\nThe detailed [model](v2-model.md) preserves the initial frozen rules and explicitly\ndated implementation discoveries. [Process](v2-process.md) records reconstruction,\nablation, controls and the projection map. No new lifecycle model or generic\ncodec framework was introduced.\n"}
